 "kind": "inhibitory knot (k=7)",
 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d038: inhibitory knot (k=7)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.5 and radius 1.64 with 7 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.35) and a 4-dim charge; 8 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 16 couplings (88% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.99 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.03 bonds per particle, largest molecule 6.0 particles, 11.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.106 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.105  random bonds 0.112\n  xor      0.568 vs ESN 0.514\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.599  random bonds 0.557\n  narma10  0.238 vs ESN 0.844\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.232  random bonds 0.243\n  latch    0.903 vs ESN 0.678\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.885  random bonds 0.895\n  mnist14  0.538 vs ESN 0.830\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.512  random bonds 0.544\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
